{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"React Query is now written in "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TypeScript"}]},{"type":"text","value":" to make sure the library and your projects are type-safe!"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Things to keep in mind:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Query follows "},{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/DefinitelyTyped/DefinitelyTyped#support-window","children":[{"type":"text","value":"DefinitelyTyped's support window"}]},{"type":"text","value":" and supports TypeScript versions released within the last 2 years. At the moment, that means TypeScript "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"5.6"}]},{"type":"text","value":" and newer."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Changes to types in this repository are considered "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"non-breaking"}]},{"type":"text","value":" and are usually released as "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"patch"}]},{"type":"text","value":" semver changes (otherwise every type enhancement would be a major version!)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"It is "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"highly recommended that you lock your react-query package version to a specific patch release and upgrade with the expectation that types may be fixed or upgraded between any release"}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The non-type-related public API of React Query still follows semver very strictly."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"type-inference","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Type Inference"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Types in React Query generally flow through very well so that you don't have to provide type annotations for yourself"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const { data } = useQuery({\n  //    ^? const data: number | undefined\n  queryKey: ['test'],\n  queryFn: () => Promise.resolve(5),\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://www.typescriptlang.org/play?#code/JYWwDg9gTgLgBAbzgVwM4FMCKz1QJ5wC+cAZlBCHAORToCGAxjALQCOO+VAsAFC8MQAdqnhIAJnRh0icALwoM2XHgAUAbSqDkIAEa4qAXQA0cFQEo5APjgAFciGAYAdLVQQANgDd0KgKxmzXgB6ILgw8IA9AH5eIA","children":[{"type":"text","value":"typescript playground"}]}]},{"type":"code","value":"const { data } = useQuery({\n  //      ^? const data: string | undefined\n  queryKey: ['test'],\n  queryFn: () => Promise.resolve(5),\n  select: (data) => data.toString(),\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://www.typescriptlang.org/play?#code/JYWwDg9gTgLgBAbzgVwM4FMCKz1QJ5wC+cAZlBCHAORToCGAxjALQCOO+VAsAFC8MQAdqnhIAJnRh0icALwoM2XHgAUAbSox0IqgF0ANHBUBKOQD44ABXIhgGAHS1UEADYA3dCoCsxw0gwu6EwAXHASUuZhknT2MBAAyjBQwIIA5iaExrwA9Nlw+QUAegD8vEA","children":[{"type":"text","value":"typescript playground"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This works best if your "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryFn"},{"type":"text","value":" has a well-defined returned type. Keep in mind that most data fetching libraries return "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"any"},{"type":"text","value":" per default, so make sure to extract it to a properly typed function:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const fetchGroups = (): Promise<Group[]> =>\n  axios.get('/groups').then((response) => response.data)\n\nconst { data } = useQuery({ queryKey: ['groups'], queryFn: fetchGroups })\n//      ^? const data: Group[] | undefined","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://www.typescriptlang.org/play?#","children":[{"type":"text","value":"typescript playground"}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"type-narrowing","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Type Narrowing"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"React Query uses a "},{"type":"link","href":"https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/typescript-in-5-minutes-func.html#discriminated-unions","children":[{"type":"text","value":"discriminated union type"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for the query result, discriminated by the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"status"},{"type":"text","value":" field and the derived status boolean flags. This will allow you to check for e.g. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"success"},{"type":"text","value":" status to make "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":" defined:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const { data, isSuccess } = useQuery({\n  queryKey: ['test'],\n  queryFn: () => Promise.resolve(5),\n})\n\nif (isSuccess) {\n  data\n  //  ^? const data: number\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://www.typescriptlang.org/play?#code/JYWwDg9gTgLgBAbzgVwM4FMCKz1QJ5wC+cAZlBCHAORToCGAxjALQCOO+VAsAFC8MQAdqnhIAJnRh0ANHGCoAysgYN0qVETgBeFBmy48ACgDaVGGphUAurMMBKbQD44ABXIh56AHS1UEADYAbuiGAKx2dry8wCRwhvJKKmqoDgi8cBlwElK8APS5GQB6APy8hLxAA","children":[{"type":"text","value":"typescript playground"}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"typing-the-error-field","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Typing the error field"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The type for error defaults to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Error"},{"type":"text","value":", because that is what most users expect."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const { error } = useQuery({ queryKey: ['groups'], queryFn: fetchGroups })\n//      ^? const error: Error","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://www.typescriptlang.org/play?#code/JYWwDg9gTgLgBAbzgVwM4FMCKz1QJ5wC+cAZlBCHAOQACMAhgHaoMDGA1gPRTr2swBaAI458VALAAoUJFhx6AD2ARUpcpSqLlqCZKkw8YdHADi5ZGDgBeRHGAATAFxxGyEACNcRKVNYRm8CToMKwAFmYQFqo2ABQAlM4ACurAGAA8ERYA2gC6AHzWBVoqAHQA5sExVJxl5mA6cSUwoeiMMTyokMzGVgUdXRgl9vQMcT6SfgG2uORQRNYoGNi4eDFZVLWR9VQ5ADSkwWGZ9WOSnJxwl1cAegD8QA","children":[{"type":"text","value":"typescript playground"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you want to throw a custom error, or something that isn't an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Error"},{"type":"text","value":" at all, you can specify the type of the error field:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const { error } = useQuery<Group[], string>(['groups'], fetchGroups)\n//      ^? const error: string | null","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"However, this has the drawback that type inference for all other generics of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useQuery"},{"type":"text","value":" will not work anymore. It is generally not considered a good practice to throw something that isn't an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Error"},{"type":"text","value":", so if you have a subclass like "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"AxiosError"},{"type":"text","value":" you can use "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"type narrowing"}]},{"type":"text","value":" to make the error field more specific:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import axios from 'axios'\n\nconst { error } = useQuery({ queryKey: ['groups'], queryFn: fetchGroups })\n//      ^? const error: Error | null\n\nif (axios.isAxiosError(error)) {\n  error\n  // ^? const error: AxiosError\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://www.typescriptlang.org/play?#-OPAEhwGLFVAlVIAQSUKgAolBZjEZtA4nFEFJPkioOi4O84H8pIQgA","children":[{"type":"text","value":"typescript playground"}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"registering-a-global-error","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Registering a global Error"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Query v5 allows for a way to set a global Error type for everything, without having to specify generics on call-sides, by amending the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Register"},{"type":"text","value":" interface. This will make sure inference still works, but the error field will be of the specified type. If you want to enforce that call-sides must do explicit type-narrowing, set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"defaultError"},{"type":"text","value":" to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"unknown"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import '@tanstack/react-query'\n\ndeclare module '@tanstack/react-query' {\n  interface Register {\n    // Use unknown so call sites must narrow explicitly.\n    defaultError: unknown\n  }\n}\n\nconst { error } = useQuery({ queryKey: ['groups'], queryFn: fetchGroups })\n//      ^? const error: unknown | null","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"typing-meta","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Typing meta"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"registering-global-meta","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Registering global Meta"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Similarly to registering a "},{"type":"link","href":"#registering-a-global-error","children":[{"type":"text","value":"global error type"}]},{"type":"text","value":" you can also register a global "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Meta"},{"type":"text","value":" type. This ensures the optional "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"meta"},{"type":"text","value":" field on "},{"type":"link","href":"./reference/functions/useQuery.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"queries"}]},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"link","href":"./reference/functions/useMutation.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"mutations"}]},{"type":"text","value":" stays consistent and is type-safe. Note that the registered type must extend "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Record<string, unknown>"},{"type":"text","value":" so that "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"meta"},{"type":"text","value":" remains an object."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import '@tanstack/react-query'\n\ninterface MyMeta extends Record<string, unknown> {\n  // Your meta type definition.\n}\n\ndeclare module '@tanstack/react-query' {\n  interface Register {\n    queryMeta: MyMeta\n    mutationMeta: MyMeta\n  }\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"typing-query-and-mutation-keys","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Typing query and mutation keys"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"registering-the-query-and-mutation-key-types","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Registering the query and mutation key types"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Also similarly to registering a "},{"type":"link","href":"#registering-a-global-error","children":[{"type":"text","value":"global error type"}]},{"type":"text","value":", you can also register a global "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"QueryKey"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"MutationKey"},{"type":"text","value":" type. This allows you to provide more structure to your keys, that matches your application's hierarchy, and have them be typed across all of the library's surface area. Note that the registered type must extend the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Array"},{"type":"text","value":" type, so that your keys remain an array."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import '@tanstack/react-query'\n\ntype QueryKey = ['dashboard' | 'marketing', ...ReadonlyArray<unknown>]\n\ndeclare module '@tanstack/react-query' {\n  interface Register {\n    queryKey: QueryKey\n    mutationKey: QueryKey\n  }\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"typing-query-options","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Typing Query Options"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you inline query options into "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useQuery"},{"type":"text","value":", you'll get automatic type inference. However, you might want to extract the query options into a separate function to share them between "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useQuery"},{"type":"text","value":" and e.g. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"query"},{"type":"text","value":". In that case, you'd lose type inference. To get it back, you can use the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryOptions"},{"type":"text","value":" helper:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { queryOptions } from '@tanstack/react-query'\n\nfunction groupOptions() {\n  return queryOptions({\n    queryKey: ['groups'],\n    queryFn: fetchGroups,\n    staleTime: 5 * 1000,\n  })\n}\n\nuseQuery(groupOptions())\nqueryClient.query(groupOptions())","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Further, the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryKey"},{"type":"text","value":" returned from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryOptions"},{"type":"text","value":" knows about the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryFn"},{"type":"text","value":" associated with it, and we can leverage that type information to make functions like "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryClient.getQueryData"},{"type":"text","value":" aware of those types as well:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"function groupOptions() {\n  return queryOptions({\n    queryKey: ['groups'],\n    queryFn: fetchGroups,\n    staleTime: 5 * 1000,\n  })\n}\n\nconst data = queryClient.getQueryData(groupOptions().queryKey)\n//     ^? const data: Group[] | undefined","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Without "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryOptions"},{"type":"text","value":", the type of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":" would be "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"unknown"},{"type":"text","value":", unless we'd pass a generic to it:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const data = queryClient.getQueryData<Group[]>(['groups'])","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Note that type inference via "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryOptions"},{"type":"text","value":" does "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"not"}]},{"type":"text","value":" work for "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryClient.getQueriesData"},{"type":"text","value":", because it returns an array of tuples with heterogeneous, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"unknown"},{"type":"text","value":" data. If you are sure of the type of data that your query will return, specify it explicitly:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const entries = queryClient.getQueriesData<Group[]>(groupOptions().queryKey)\n//     ^? const entries: Array<[QueryKey, Group[] | undefined]>","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"typing-mutation-options","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Typing Mutation Options"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Similarly to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryOptions"},{"type":"text","value":", you can use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"mutationOptions"},{"type":"text","value":" to extract mutation options into a separate function:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"function groupMutationOptions() {\n  return mutationOptions({\n    mutationKey: ['addGroup'],\n    mutationFn: addGroup,\n  })\n}\n\nuseMutation({\n  ...groupMutationOptions(),\n  onSuccess: () => queryClient.invalidateQueries({ queryKey: ['groups'] }),\n})\nuseIsMutating(groupMutationOptions())\nqueryClient.isMutating(groupMutationOptions())","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"typesafe-disabling-of-queries-using-skiptoken","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Typesafe disabling of queries using "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"skipToken"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you are using TypeScript, you can use the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"skipToken"},{"type":"text","value":" to disable a query. This is useful when you want to disable a query based on a condition, but you still want to keep the query to be type safe.\nRead more about it in the "},{"type":"link","href":"./guides/disabling-queries.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Disabling Queries"}]},{"type":"text","value":" guide."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"further-reading","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Further Reading"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For tips and tricks around type inference, see the article "},{"type":"link","href":"https://tkdodo.eu/blog/react-query-and-type-script","children":[{"type":"text","value":"React Query and TypeScript"}]},{"type":"text","value":". To find out how to get the best possible type-safety, you can read "},{"type":"link","href":"https://tkdodo.eu/blog/type-safe-react-query","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Type-safe React Query"}]},{"type":"text","value":". "},{"type":"link","href":"https://tkdodo.eu/blog/the-query-options-api","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The Query Options API"}]},{"type":"text","value":" outlines how type inference works with the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryOptions"},{"type":"text","value":" helper function."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"type-inference","text":"Type Inference","level":2},{"id":"type-narrowing","text":"Type Narrowing","level":2},{"id":"typing-the-error-field","text":"Typing the error field","level":2},{"id":"registering-a-global-error","text":"Registering a global Error","level":3},{"id":"typing-meta","text":"Typing meta","level":2},{"id":"registering-global-meta","text":"Registering global Meta","level":3},{"id":"typing-query-and-mutation-keys","text":"Typing query and mutation keys","level":2},{"id":"registering-the-query-and-mutation-key-types","text":"Registering the query and mutation key types","level":3},{"id":"typing-query-options","text":"Typing Query Options","level":2},{"id":"typing-mutation-options","text":"Typing Mutation Options","level":2},{"id":"typesafe-disabling-of-queries-using-skiptoken","text":"Typesafe disabling of queries using skipToken","level":2},{"id":"further-reading","text":"Further Reading","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nReact Query is now written in **TypeScript** to make sure the library and your projects are type-safe!\n\nThings to keep in mind:\n\n- TanStack Query follows [DefinitelyTyped's support window](https://github.com/DefinitelyTyped/DefinitelyTyped#support-window) and supports TypeScript versions released within the last 2 years. At the moment, that means TypeScript **5.6** and newer.\n- Changes to types in this repository are considered **non-breaking** and are usually released as **patch** semver changes (otherwise every type enhancement would be a major version!).\n- It is **highly recommended that you lock your react-query package version to a specific patch release and upgrade with the expectation that types may be fixed or upgraded between any release**\n- The non-type-related public API of React Query still follows semver very strictly.\n\n## Type Inference\n\nTypes in React Query generally flow through very well so that you don't have to provide type annotations for yourself\n\n[//]: # 'TypeInference1'\n\n```tsx\nconst { data } = useQuery({\n  //    ^? const data: number | undefined\n  queryKey: ['test'],\n  queryFn: () => Promise.resolve(5),\n})\n```\n\n[typescript playground](https://www.typescriptlang.org/play?#code/JYWwDg9gTgLgBAbzgVwM4FMCKz1QJ5wC+cAZlBCHAORToCGAxjALQCOO+VAsAFC8MQAdqnhIAJnRh0icALwoM2XHgAUAbSqDkIAEa4qAXQA0cFQEo5APjgAFciGAYAdLVQQANgDd0KgKxmzXgB6ILgw8IA9AH5eIA)\n\n[//]: # 'TypeInference1'\n[//]: # 'TypeInference2'\n\n```tsx\nconst { data } = useQuery({\n  //      ^? const data: string | undefined\n  queryKey: ['test'],\n  queryFn: () => Promise.resolve(5),\n  select: (data) => data.toString(),\n})\n```\n\n[typescript playground](https://www.typescriptlang.org/play?#code/JYWwDg9gTgLgBAbzgVwM4FMCKz1QJ5wC+cAZlBCHAORToCGAxjALQCOO+VAsAFC8MQAdqnhIAJnRh0icALwoM2XHgAUAbSox0IqgF0ANHBUBKOQD44ABXIhgGAHS1UEADYA3dCoCsxw0gwu6EwAXHASUuZhknT2MBAAyjBQwIIA5iaExrwA9Nlw+QUAegD8vEA)\n\n[//]: # 'TypeInference2'\n\nThis works best if your `queryFn` has a well-defined returned type. Keep in mind that most data fetching libraries return `any` per default, so make sure to extract it to a properly typed function:\n\n[//]: # 'TypeInference3'\n\n```tsx\nconst fetchGroups = (): Promise<Group[]> =>\n  axios.get('/groups').then((response) => response.data)\n\nconst { data } = useQuery({ queryKey: ['groups'], queryFn: fetchGroups })\n//      ^? const data: Group[] | undefined\n```\n\n[typescript playground](https://www.typescriptlang.org/play?#)\n\n[//]: # 'TypeInference3'\n\n## Type Narrowing\n\nReact Query uses a [discriminated union type](https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/typescript-in-5-minutes-func.html#discriminated-unions) for the query result, discriminated by the `status` field and the derived status boolean flags. This will allow you to check for e.g. `success` status to make `data` defined:\n\n[//]: # 'TypeNarrowing'\n\n```tsx\nconst { data, isSuccess } = useQuery({\n  queryKey: ['test'],\n  queryFn: () => Promise.resolve(5),\n})\n\nif (isSuccess) {\n  data\n  //  ^? const data: number\n}\n```\n\n[typescript playground](https://www.typescriptlang.org/play?#code/JYWwDg9gTgLgBAbzgVwM4FMCKz1QJ5wC+cAZlBCHAORToCGAxjALQCOO+VAsAFC8MQAdqnhIAJnRh0ANHGCoAysgYN0qVETgBeFBmy48ACgDaVGGphUAurMMBKbQD44ABXIh56AHS1UEADYAbuiGAKx2dry8wCRwhvJKKmqoDgi8cBlwElK8APS5GQB6APy8hLxAA)\n\n[//]: # 'TypeNarrowing'\n\n## Typing the error field\n\nThe type for error defaults to `Error`, because that is what most users expect.\n\n[//]: # 'TypingError'\n\n```tsx\nconst { error } = useQuery({ queryKey: ['groups'], queryFn: fetchGroups })\n//      ^? const error: Error\n```\n\n[typescript playground](https://www.typescriptlang.org/play?#code/JYWwDg9gTgLgBAbzgVwM4FMCKz1QJ5wC+cAZlBCHAOQACMAhgHaoMDGA1gPRTr2swBaAI458VALAAoUJFhx6AD2ARUpcpSqLlqCZKkw8YdHADi5ZGDgBeRHGAATAFxxGyEACNcRKVNYRm8CToMKwAFmYQFqo2ABQAlM4ACurAGAA8ERYA2gC6AHzWBVoqAHQA5sExVJxl5mA6cSUwoeiMMTyokMzGVgUdXRgl9vQMcT6SfgG2uORQRNYoGNi4eDFZVLWR9VQ5ADSkwWGZ9WOSnJxwl1cAegD8QA)\n\n[//]: # 'TypingError'\n\nIf you want to throw a custom error, or something that isn't an `Error` at all, you can specify the type of the error field:\n\n[//]: # 'TypingError2'\n\n```tsx\nconst { error } = useQuery<Group[], string>(['groups'], fetchGroups)\n//      ^? const error: string | null\n```\n\n[//]: # 'TypingError2'\n\nHowever, this has the drawback that type inference for all other generics of `useQuery` will not work anymore. It is generally not considered a good practice to throw something that isn't an `Error`, so if you have a subclass like `AxiosError` you can use _type narrowing_ to make the error field more specific:\n\n[//]: # 'TypingError3'\n\n```tsx\nimport axios from 'axios'\n\nconst { error } = useQuery({ queryKey: ['groups'], queryFn: fetchGroups })\n//      ^? const error: Error | null\n\nif (axios.isAxiosError(error)) {\n  error\n  // ^? const error: AxiosError\n}\n```\n\n[typescript playground](https://www.typescriptlang.org/play?#-OPAEhwGLFVAlVIAQSUKgAolBZjEZtA4nFEFJPkioOi4O84H8pIQgA)\n\n[//]: # 'TypingError3'\n\n### Registering a global Error\n\nTanStack Query v5 allows for a way to set a global Error type for everything, without having to specify generics on call-sides, by amending the `Register` interface. This will make sure inference still works, but the error field will be of the specified type. If you want to enforce that call-sides must do explicit type-narrowing, set `defaultError` to `unknown`:\n\n[//]: # 'RegisterErrorType'\n\n```tsx\nimport '@tanstack/react-query'\n\ndeclare module '@tanstack/react-query' {\n  interface Register {\n    // Use unknown so call sites must narrow explicitly.\n    defaultError: unknown\n  }\n}\n\nconst { error } = useQuery({ queryKey: ['groups'], queryFn: fetchGroups })\n//      ^? const error: unknown | null\n```\n\n[//]: # 'RegisterErrorType'\n[//]: # 'TypingMeta'\n\n## Typing meta\n\n### Registering global Meta\n\nSimilarly to registering a [global error type](#registering-a-global-error) you can also register a global `Meta` type. This ensures the optional `meta` field on [queries](./reference/functions/useQuery.md) and [mutations](./reference/functions/useMutation.md) stays consistent and is type-safe. Note that the registered type must extend `Record<string, unknown>` so that `meta` remains an object.\n\n```ts\nimport '@tanstack/react-query'\n\ninterface MyMeta extends Record<string, unknown> {\n  // Your meta type definition.\n}\n\ndeclare module '@tanstack/react-query' {\n  interface Register {\n    queryMeta: MyMeta\n    mutationMeta: MyMeta\n  }\n}\n```\n\n[//]: # 'TypingMeta'\n[//]: # 'TypingQueryAndMutationKeys'\n\n## Typing query and mutation keys\n\n### Registering the query and mutation key types\n\nAlso similarly to registering a [global error type](#registering-a-global-error), you can also register a global `QueryKey` and `MutationKey` type. This allows you to provide more structure to your keys, that matches your application's hierarchy, and have them be typed across all of the library's surface area. Note that the registered type must extend the `Array` type, so that your keys remain an array.\n\n```ts\nimport '@tanstack/react-query'\n\ntype QueryKey = ['dashboard' | 'marketing', ...ReadonlyArray<unknown>]\n\ndeclare module '@tanstack/react-query' {\n  interface Register {\n    queryKey: QueryKey\n    mutationKey: QueryKey\n  }\n}\n```\n\n[//]: # 'TypingQueryAndMutationKeys'\n[//]: # 'TypingQueryOptions'\n\n## Typing Query Options\n\nIf you inline query options into `useQuery`, you'll get automatic type inference. However, you might want to extract the query options into a separate function to share them between `useQuery` and e.g. `query`. In that case, you'd lose type inference. To get it back, you can use the `queryOptions` helper:\n\n```ts\nimport { queryOptions } from '@tanstack/react-query'\n\nfunction groupOptions() {\n  return queryOptions({\n    queryKey: ['groups'],\n    queryFn: fetchGroups,\n    staleTime: 5 * 1000,\n  })\n}\n\nuseQuery(groupOptions())\nqueryClient.query(groupOptions())\n```\n\nFurther, the `queryKey` returned from `queryOptions` knows about the `queryFn` associated with it, and we can leverage that type information to make functions like `queryClient.getQueryData` aware of those types as well:\n\n```ts\nfunction groupOptions() {\n  return queryOptions({\n    queryKey: ['groups'],\n    queryFn: fetchGroups,\n    staleTime: 5 * 1000,\n  })\n}\n\nconst data = queryClient.getQueryData(groupOptions().queryKey)\n//     ^? const data: Group[] | undefined\n```\n\nWithout `queryOptions`, the type of `data` would be `unknown`, unless we'd pass a generic to it:\n\n```ts\nconst data = queryClient.getQueryData<Group[]>(['groups'])\n```\n\nNote that type inference via `queryOptions` does _not_ work for `queryClient.getQueriesData`, because it returns an array of tuples with heterogeneous, `unknown` data. If you are sure of the type of data that your query will return, specify it explicitly:\n\n```ts\nconst entries = queryClient.getQueriesData<Group[]>(groupOptions().queryKey)\n//     ^? const entries: Array<[QueryKey, Group[] | undefined]>\n```\n\n## Typing Mutation Options\n\nSimilarly to `queryOptions`, you can use `mutationOptions` to extract mutation options into a separate function:\n\n```ts\nfunction groupMutationOptions() {\n  return mutationOptions({\n    mutationKey: ['addGroup'],\n    mutationFn: addGroup,\n  })\n}\n\nuseMutation({\n  ...groupMutationOptions(),\n  onSuccess: () => queryClient.invalidateQueries({ queryKey: ['groups'] }),\n})\nuseIsMutating(groupMutationOptions())\nqueryClient.isMutating(groupMutationOptions())\n```\n\n[//]: # 'TypingQueryOptions'\n\n## Typesafe disabling of queries using `skipToken`\n\nIf you are using TypeScript, you can use the `skipToken` to disable a query. This is useful when you want to disable a query based on a condition, but you still want to keep the query to be type safe.\nRead more about it in the [Disabling Queries](./guides/disabling-queries.md) guide.\n\n[//]: # 'Materials'\n\n## Further Reading\n\nFor tips and tricks around type inference, see the article [React Query and TypeScript](https://tkdodo.eu/blog/react-query-and-type-script). To find out how to get the best possible type-safety, you can read [Type-safe React Query](https://tkdodo.eu/blog/type-safe-react-query). [The Query Options API](https://tkdodo.eu/blog/the-query-options-api) outlines how type inference works with the `queryOptions` helper function.\n\n[//]: # 'Materials'\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\nid: typescript\ntitle: TypeScript\n---\n\nReact Query is now written in **TypeScript** to make sure the library and your projects are type-safe!\n\nThings to keep in mind:\n\n- TanStack Query follows [DefinitelyTyped's support window](https://github.com/DefinitelyTyped/DefinitelyTyped#support-window) and supports TypeScript versions released within the last 2 years. At the moment, that means TypeScript **5.6** and newer.\n- Changes to types in this repository are considered **non-breaking** and are usually released as **patch** semver changes (otherwise every type enhancement would be a major version!).\n- It is **highly recommended that you lock your react-query package version to a specific patch release and upgrade with the expectation that types may be fixed or upgraded between any release**\n- The non-type-related public API of React Query still follows semver very strictly.\n\n## Type Inference\n\nTypes in React Query generally flow through very well so that you don't have to provide type annotations for yourself\n\n[//]: # 'TypeInference1'\n\n```tsx\nconst { data } = useQuery({\n  //    ^? const data: number | undefined\n  queryKey: ['test'],\n  queryFn: () => Promise.resolve(5),\n})\n```\n\n[typescript playground](https://www.typescriptlang.org/play?#code/JYWwDg9gTgLgBAbzgVwM4FMCKz1QJ5wC+cAZlBCHAORToCGAxjALQCOO+VAsAFC8MQAdqnhIAJnRh0icALwoM2XHgAUAbSqDkIAEa4qAXQA0cFQEo5APjgAFciGAYAdLVQQANgDd0KgKxmzXgB6ILgw8IA9AH5eIA)\n\n[//]: # 'TypeInference1'\n[//]: # 'TypeInference2'\n\n```tsx\nconst { data } = useQuery({\n  //      ^? const data: string | undefined\n  queryKey: ['test'],\n  queryFn: () => Promise.resolve(5),\n  select: (data) => data.toString(),\n})\n```\n\n[typescript playground](https://www.typescriptlang.org/play?#code/JYWwDg9gTgLgBAbzgVwM4FMCKz1QJ5wC+cAZlBCHAORToCGAxjALQCOO+VAsAFC8MQAdqnhIAJnRh0icALwoM2XHgAUAbSox0IqgF0ANHBUBKOQD44ABXIhgGAHS1UEADYA3dCoCsxw0gwu6EwAXHASUuZhknT2MBAAyjBQwIIA5iaExrwA9Nlw+QUAegD8vEA)\n\n[//]: # 'TypeInference2'\n\nThis works best if your `queryFn` has a well-defined returned type. Keep in mind that most data fetching libraries return `any` per default, so make sure to extract it to a properly typed function:\n\n[//]: # 'TypeInference3'\n\n```tsx\nconst fetchGroups = (): Promise<Group[]> =>\n  axios.get('/groups').then((response) => response.data)\n\nconst { data } = useQuery({ queryKey: ['groups'], queryFn: fetchGroups })\n//      ^? const data: Group[] | undefined\n```\n\n[typescript playground](https://www.typescriptlang.org/play?#)\n\n[//]: # 'TypeInference3'\n\n## Type Narrowing\n\nReact Query uses a [discriminated union type](https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/typescript-in-5-minutes-func.html#discriminated-unions) for the query result, discriminated by the `status` field and the derived status boolean flags. This will allow you to check for e.g. `success` status to make `data` defined:\n\n[//]: # 'TypeNarrowing'\n\n```tsx\nconst { data, isSuccess } = useQuery({\n  queryKey: ['test'],\n  queryFn: () => Promise.resolve(5),\n})\n\nif (isSuccess) {\n  data\n  //  ^? const data: number\n}\n```\n\n[typescript playground](https://www.typescriptlang.org/play?#code/JYWwDg9gTgLgBAbzgVwM4FMCKz1QJ5wC+cAZlBCHAORToCGAxjALQCOO+VAsAFC8MQAdqnhIAJnRh0ANHGCoAysgYN0qVETgBeFBmy48ACgDaVGGphUAurMMBKbQD44ABXIh56AHS1UEADYAbuiGAKx2dry8wCRwhvJKKmqoDgi8cBlwElK8APS5GQB6APy8hLxAA)\n\n[//]: # 'TypeNarrowing'\n\n## Typing the error field\n\nThe type for error defaults to `Error`, because that is what most users expect.\n\n[//]: # 'TypingError'\n\n```tsx\nconst { error } = useQuery({ queryKey: ['groups'], queryFn: fetchGroups })\n//      ^? const error: Error\n```\n\n[typescript playground](https://www.typescriptlang.org/play?#code/JYWwDg9gTgLgBAbzgVwM4FMCKz1QJ5wC+cAZlBCHAOQACMAhgHaoMDGA1gPRTr2swBaAI458VALAAoUJFhx6AD2ARUpcpSqLlqCZKkw8YdHADi5ZGDgBeRHGAATAFxxGyEACNcRKVNYRm8CToMKwAFmYQFqo2ABQAlM4ACurAGAA8ERYA2gC6AHzWBVoqAHQA5sExVJxl5mA6cSUwoeiMMTyokMzGVgUdXRgl9vQMcT6SfgG2uORQRNYoGNi4eDFZVLWR9VQ5ADSkwWGZ9WOSnJxwl1cAegD8QA)\n\n[//]: # 'TypingError'\n\nIf you want to throw a custom error, or something that isn't an `Error` at all, you can specify the type of the error field:\n\n[//]: # 'TypingError2'\n\n```tsx\nconst { error } = useQuery<Group[], string>(['groups'], fetchGroups)\n//      ^? const error: string | null\n```\n\n[//]: # 'TypingError2'\n\nHowever, this has the drawback that type inference for all other generics of `useQuery` will not work anymore. It is generally not considered a good practice to throw something that isn't an `Error`, so if you have a subclass like `AxiosError` you can use _type narrowing_ to make the error field more specific:\n\n[//]: # 'TypingError3'\n\n```tsx\nimport axios from 'axios'\n\nconst { error } = useQuery({ queryKey: ['groups'], queryFn: fetchGroups })\n//      ^? const error: Error | null\n\nif (axios.isAxiosError(error)) {\n  error\n  // ^? const error: AxiosError\n}\n```\n\n[typescript playground](https://www.typescriptlang.org/play?#-OPAEhwGLFVAlVIAQSUKgAolBZjEZtA4nFEFJPkioOi4O84H8pIQgA)\n\n[//]: # 'TypingError3'\n\n### Registering a global Error\n\nTanStack Query v5 allows for a way to set a global Error type for everything, without having to specify generics on call-sides, by amending the `Register` interface. This will make sure inference still works, but the error field will be of the specified type. If you want to enforce that call-sides must do explicit type-narrowing, set `defaultError` to `unknown`:\n\n[//]: # 'RegisterErrorType'\n\n```tsx\nimport '@tanstack/react-query'\n\ndeclare module '@tanstack/react-query' {\n  interface Register {\n    // Use unknown so call sites must narrow explicitly.\n    defaultError: unknown\n  }\n}\n\nconst { error } = useQuery({ queryKey: ['groups'], queryFn: fetchGroups })\n//      ^? const error: unknown | null\n```\n\n[//]: # 'RegisterErrorType'\n[//]: # 'TypingMeta'\n\n## Typing meta\n\n### Registering global Meta\n\nSimilarly to registering a [global error type](#registering-a-global-error) you can also register a global `Meta` type. This ensures the optional `meta` field on [queries](./reference/functions/useQuery.md) and [mutations](./reference/functions/useMutation.md) stays consistent and is type-safe. Note that the registered type must extend `Record<string, unknown>` so that `meta` remains an object.\n\n```ts\nimport '@tanstack/react-query'\n\ninterface MyMeta extends Record<string, unknown> {\n  // Your meta type definition.\n}\n\ndeclare module '@tanstack/react-query' {\n  interface Register {\n    queryMeta: MyMeta\n    mutationMeta: MyMeta\n  }\n}\n```\n\n[//]: # 'TypingMeta'\n[//]: # 'TypingQueryAndMutationKeys'\n\n## Typing query and mutation keys\n\n### Registering the query and mutation key types\n\nAlso similarly to registering a [global error type](#registering-a-global-error), you can also register a global `QueryKey` and `MutationKey` type. This allows you to provide more structure to your keys, that matches your application's hierarchy, and have them be typed across all of the library's surface area. Note that the registered type must extend the `Array` type, so that your keys remain an array.\n\n```ts\nimport '@tanstack/react-query'\n\ntype QueryKey = ['dashboard' | 'marketing', ...ReadonlyArray<unknown>]\n\ndeclare module '@tanstack/react-query' {\n  interface Register {\n    queryKey: QueryKey\n    mutationKey: QueryKey\n  }\n}\n```\n\n[//]: # 'TypingQueryAndMutationKeys'\n[//]: # 'TypingQueryOptions'\n\n## Typing Query Options\n\nIf you inline query options into `useQuery`, you'll get automatic type inference. However, you might want to extract the query options into a separate function to share them between `useQuery` and e.g. `query`. In that case, you'd lose type inference. To get it back, you can use the `queryOptions` helper:\n\n```ts\nimport { queryOptions } from '@tanstack/react-query'\n\nfunction groupOptions() {\n  return queryOptions({\n    queryKey: ['groups'],\n    queryFn: fetchGroups,\n    staleTime: 5 * 1000,\n  })\n}\n\nuseQuery(groupOptions())\nqueryClient.query(groupOptions())\n```\n\nFurther, the `queryKey` returned from `queryOptions` knows about the `queryFn` associated with it, and we can leverage that type information to make functions like `queryClient.getQueryData` aware of those types as well:\n\n```ts\nfunction groupOptions() {\n  return queryOptions({\n    queryKey: ['groups'],\n    queryFn: fetchGroups,\n    staleTime: 5 * 1000,\n  })\n}\n\nconst data = queryClient.getQueryData(groupOptions().queryKey)\n//     ^? const data: Group[] | undefined\n```\n\nWithout `queryOptions`, the type of `data` would be `unknown`, unless we'd pass a generic to it:\n\n```ts\nconst data = queryClient.getQueryData<Group[]>(['groups'])\n```\n\nNote that type inference via `queryOptions` does _not_ work for `queryClient.getQueriesData`, because it returns an array of tuples with heterogeneous, `unknown` data. If you are sure of the type of data that your query will return, specify it explicitly:\n\n```ts\nconst entries = queryClient.getQueriesData<Group[]>(groupOptions().queryKey)\n//     ^? const entries: Array<[QueryKey, Group[] | undefined]>\n```\n\n## Typing Mutation Options\n\nSimilarly to `queryOptions`, you can use `mutationOptions` to extract mutation options into a separate function:\n\n```ts\nfunction groupMutationOptions() {\n  return mutationOptions({\n    mutationKey: ['addGroup'],\n    mutationFn: addGroup,\n  })\n}\n\nuseMutation({\n  ...groupMutationOptions(),\n  onSuccess: () => queryClient.invalidateQueries({ queryKey: ['groups'] }),\n})\nuseIsMutating(groupMutationOptions())\nqueryClient.isMutating(groupMutationOptions())\n```\n\n[//]: # 'TypingQueryOptions'\n\n## Typesafe disabling of queries using `skipToken`\n\nIf you are using TypeScript, you can use the `skipToken` to disable a query. This is useful when you want to disable a query based on a condition, but you still want to keep the query to be type safe.\nRead more about it in the [Disabling Queries](./guides/disabling-queries.md) guide.\n\n[//]: # 'Materials'\n\n## Further Reading\n\nFor tips and tricks around type inference, see the article [React Query and TypeScript](https://tkdodo.eu/blog/react-query-and-type-script). To find out how to get the best possible type-safety, you can read [Type-safe React Query](https://tkdodo.eu/blog/type-safe-react-query). [The Query Options API](https://tkdodo.eu/blog/the-query-options-api) outlines how type inference works with the `queryOptions` helper function.\n\n[//]: # 'Materials'\n"}
